Chapter: ProcStateRunning

 The running process's state word says RUNNING. The scheduler's
 claim-before-publish protocol rests on that, and proc 0 is the one
 entry it is not true of by construction.

 A process becomes RUNNING by being claimed -- the claim is a CMPXCHG
 that moves the state word from READY to RUNNING, so every spawned
 process arrives in the right state on its own. Proc 0 is never
 spawned and never claimed: it is the program the machine booted, and
 its table entry is zeroed at boot along with the rest of the table.
 Zero is FREE. So it is initialised to RUNNING explicitly, and this
 test is what says so.

 Delete that initialisation and the failure is quiet and horrible. The
 timer's preemption publishes the process it interrupted as READY with
 a CMPXCHG from RUNNING -- which is a CMPXCHG precisely so that it
 cannot resurrect a process the max-ticks check has just zombified. If
 proc 0 sits at FREE, that compare never matches, proc 0 is never
 published, and the boot processor can never be scheduled back onto the
 program it was running. Nothing reports an error. The machine simply
 stops being the thing it was doing.

 The process table is 16 entries of 256 bytes at 20480, and the state
 word is the first field of an entry. proc-state-running is 2.

Section: Probe

  proc-state : Integer -> Integer
  proc-state (slot) = peek-qword 20480 (slot * 256)

  child-body : Integer -> Integer
  child-body (x) = poke-byte 28000 0 5

  opening : [Console, Concurrent] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("proc 0 at boot: " & show (proc-state 0))
    kid <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    w <- process-wait kid
    print-line-uni ("child ran: " & show (peek-byte 28000 0))
    print-line-uni ("proc 0 after a spawn and wait: " & show (proc-state 0))
    print-line-uni ("exited child is free: " & show (proc-state kid))
  end
